<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Boundary Integral Equation Method</title>

  <p>
    The Boundary Integral Equation Method is a numerical method for calculating the analytic capacity of <m>K</m>. It is based on the fact that the Szego kernel <m>S(z,a)</m> is the solution of the following integral equation called the Kerzman-Stein Boundary Integral Equation.
    <md>
        S(z,a) - \int_{w \in \partial K} A(z,w) S(w,a) ds = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \frac{\overline{T(z)}}{\overline{a}-\overline{z}}
    </md>
    In the above equation <m>T</m> is the complex unit tangent vector and 
    <md>
        A(z,w) = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \left( \frac{T(w)}{w-z} - \frac{\overline{T(z)}}{\overline{w}-\overline{z}} \right)
    </md>
    is called the <term>Kerzman-Stein kernel</term>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The following procedure is used to numerically solve the Kerzman-Stein Boundary Integral Equation and compute the analytic capacity of <m>K</m>.
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                First to avoid needing to perform calculations with the point at <m>\infty</m> we modify the integral equation by mapping the the unbounded component <m>\mathbb C \setminus K</m> to a bounded set <m>D</m>. This makes it so that <m>\gamma(K) = 2\pi S(0,0)</m>. where <m>S</m> is the Szego kernel for <m>D</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Suppose the boundary of <m>D</m> is nice enough that we can parametrize it by some function <m>z(t)</m> with <m>0 \leq t &lt; 2\pi</m>. We approximate the integral using the trapezoid rule with <m>n</m> equally space nodes in <m>[0,2\pi]</m>, which gives the following integral equation for the approximate solution <m>\psi_m</m>:
                <md>
                    \psi_n(t) + \frac{2\pi}{n} \sum_{j=1}^n A(\zeta(t), \zeta(t_j)) \psi_n(t_j) |\zeta'(t_j)| = \frac{1}{2\pi}
                </md>
                 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                This equation needs to hold for all <m>t \in [0,2\pi]</m>. Instead we will only make sure that it holds for the chosen <m>n</m> nodes. For <m>n</m> sufficiently large this will provide a close approximation to <m>\psi(t)</m>. Thus we seek to solve
                <md>
                    \psi_n(t_k)+ \frac{i}{n} \sum_{j=1, j \neq k}^n \left( \frac{\overline{\zeta'(t_k)}}{|\zeta'(t_k)|(\overline{\zeta(t_k)}-\overline{\zeta(t_j)})}  + \frac{\zeta'(t_j)}{|\zeta'(t_j)|(\zeta(t_j)-\zeta(t_k))}\right) |\zeta'(t_j)| \psi_n(t_j) = \frac{1}{2\pi}.
                </md>
                for <m>k = 1, \ldots, n</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                By writing this equation with vectors, we can reduce it to a system of linear equations that can be solved using Gaussian elimination to find <m>\psi_n(t)</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Finally we use <m>\psi_n(t)</m> to find <m>S(0,0)</m> and estimate the value of the analytic capacity of <m>K</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    In the code for running the algorithm to find the analytic capacity of the union disk, there is a line
  </p>

  <c>
zet= exp(1i*t); % parametrization of unit disk
    </c>



    <p>
        This represents the equation <m>z = e^{it}</m> which parametrizes the unit disk for <m>0 \leq t &lt; 2\pi</m>. If you want to find the analytic capacity of a different shape, you will need to change this to the correct parametrization.
    </p>

    <p>
        Likewise, there is a line of code that reads
    </p>

    <c>
        zetp=1i*exp(1i*t); % derivative of above
    </c>

    <p>
        This represents the derivative of the parametrization <m>z' = ie^{it}</m>. If finding the analytic capacity of a different shape you will need to change this to be the derivative of your parametrization.
    </p>
  </section>